# Build output. Nothing under here is an input to anything.
/target

# The same, for the fuzz crate, which is its own workspace and so has its own
# build directory. Written out rather than folded into the line above, because
# that one is anchored at the root and would not reach this.
/fuzz/target

# What a fuzz run writes when it finds something: the input that reproduced it,
# under a name derived from its digest. It is deliberately not tracked here.
# docs/TESTING.md says what happens to one instead: it becomes a fixture with the
# repair, under a name that says what it is, where the default suite reads it.
# An artefact directory tracked as it stands would accumulate reproducers nobody
# had turned into a test.
/fuzz/artifacts

# Cargo.lock is deliberately absent from this file, and now it is tracked as
# well. It is not build output: it is the record of which versions a build
# actually resolved, and a reconstruction has to be reproducible from the same
# input years later. Ignoring it would leave every clone free to resolve
# something else and call the result the same program. What refuses that is
# --locked on the build, test and lint jobs in .github/workflows/ci.yml.

# Raw profiles from a coverage run. cargo llvm-cov keeps its own under target,
# but the end-to-end test starts the built binary with an empty environment,
# which drops the variable naming that path, so the instrumented child writes one
# beside its own manifest instead. They are output, they are large, and an
# untracked one makes the tree read as modified, which is a value the artefact
# reports. docs/TESTING.md says where the coverage command puts things.
*.profraw
